WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:05.120
Three decades of regret? Eddie Murphy&nbsp;
turned down playing a different Eddie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.120 --> 00:00:15.920
in one of Disney's greatest cult classics.&nbsp;
Why? He thought the movie was just too weird.

00:00:15.920 --> 00:00:16.480
"Hey! Eddie, take it easy, will ya? Please, Eddie!&nbsp;
Don't throw me out, you're making a big mistake!"

00:00:16.480 --> 00:00:21.840
Eddie Murphy is as iconic as his movies. The&nbsp;
actor and comedian is legendary thanks to films&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.840 --> 00:00:26.560
like "Coming to America," "Beverly Hills Cop,"&nbsp;
and "Shrek." But even though Murphy has played&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.560 --> 00:00:31.520
some of the most recognizable roles in Hollywood,&nbsp;
he's also had the bad luck of playing some of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.520 --> 00:00:36.720
worst. The actor has been nominated for nine&nbsp;
Razzie awards, and managed to take home three,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.720 --> 00:00:40.800
thanks in part to the likes of "Norbit"&nbsp;
and "The Adventures of Pluto Nash."

00:00:40.800 --> 00:00:45.440
Those flops didn't exactly help his career,&nbsp;
but thanks to "Coming to America 2" and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.440 --> 00:00:50.960
2019's "Dolemite Is My Name," the actor&nbsp;
has once again entered the limelight.

00:00:50.960 --> 00:00:52.960
"What a totally unexpected surprise."

00:00:53.600 --> 00:00:58.240
It's almost hard to believe the number of&nbsp;
recognizable roles the actor has to his name,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.240 --> 00:01:01.040
but one more could've been added to that list.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.040 --> 00:01:04.800
What movie does Murphy still regret&nbsp;
turning down after all these years?

00:01:04.800 --> 00:01:09.760
You might be surprised to learn that it was none&nbsp;
other than the groundbreaking "Who Framed Roger&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.760 --> 00:01:15.200
Rabbit." Released in 1988, "Who Framed Roger&nbsp;
Rabbit" is a bold film that seamlessly blends&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.200 --> 00:01:20.800
live-action and animation into an engaging story&nbsp;
that still holds up against modern standards.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.800 --> 00:01:26.080
The movie stars Bob Hoskins as Eddie Valiant, a&nbsp;
private detective who gets drawn into a murder&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.080 --> 00:01:31.520
investigation involving Roger Rabbit. The movie&nbsp;
was an unmitigated success -- it currently holds&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.520 --> 00:01:39.840
a 97% on Rotten Tomatoes and earned over $154&nbsp;
million domestically against a $70 million budget.

00:01:39.840 --> 00:01:43.200
"So tell me, Eddie, is that a rabbit in your&nbsp;
pocket or are you just happy to see me?"

00:01:44.240 --> 00:01:48.000
While speaking with Jimmy Fallon in&nbsp;
late 2019, Eddie Murphy said that he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:51.840
was offered the starring role of Eddie&nbsp;
Valiant. He passed on the film because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.840 --> 00:01:56.560
the concept was so unusual that he didn't&nbsp;
think it had any chance of being a success.

00:01:56.560 --> 00:02:01.400
"And I was like, what? Animation and&nbsp;
people? That sounds like bulls--- to me. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.120 --> 00:02:05.920
now every time I see it, I feel like an idiot."

00:02:05.920 --> 00:02:10.080
With Murphy in the lead role, "Who Framed Roger&nbsp;
Rabbit would've undoubtedly been a very different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.080 --> 00:02:14.720
movie than the one we know now. But that's just&nbsp;
the way the business goes. Hollywood is filled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.720 --> 00:02:19.520
with countless "What if" stories. Actors turn&nbsp;
down roles, or back out for various reasons.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.520 --> 00:02:23.600
And as it turns out, Murphy more than&nbsp;
has his fair share of these stories.

00:02:23.600 --> 00:02:28.400
In addition to missing out on "Who Framed Roger&nbsp;
Rabbit," Murphy most notably lost the chance to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.400 --> 00:02:33.680
star in 1984's "Ghostbusters." Murphy could've&nbsp;
played Winston, but he instead opted to star&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.680 --> 00:02:38.320
in "Beverly Hills Cop." It proved to be a&nbsp;
fruitful move; rather than feature as one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.320 --> 00:02:43.600
person in an ensemble, Murphy had the chance to&nbsp;
lead a film that went on to spawn two sequels.

00:02:43.600 --> 00:02:46.080
But that wasn't necessarily a strategic decision&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.080 --> 00:02:50.080
on Murphy's part. The actor claimed in&nbsp;
an interview with Extra in 2003 that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.080 --> 00:02:54.080
like "Roger Rabbit," he also turned down&nbsp;
"Ghostbusters" because he didn't see the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.080 --> 00:02:59.280
premise having any chance of success. As he put&nbsp;
it at the time, quote, "This sounds like a crock."

00:02:59.280 --> 00:03:02.480
The actor also passed on starring&nbsp;
as Detective Carter in the hit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.480 --> 00:03:06.960
action-comedy "Rush Hour" to instead&nbsp;
star in the widely forgotten "Holy Man."

00:03:07.520 --> 00:03:08.800
"I guess that's a good way to go."

00:03:09.680 --> 00:03:15.040
"Rush Hour" would spawn several sequels, while&nbsp;
"Holy Man" was a box office and critical bomb.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.040 --> 00:03:19.760
In an interview with Extra, Murphy dished on the&nbsp;
process with his usual sense of humor, saying:

00:03:19.760 --> 00:03:21.920
"It's funny when I think of it now. It was like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.920 --> 00:03:25.600
'You can go jump all over California&nbsp;
with Jackie Chan or go to Miami and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.600 --> 00:03:29.760
wear a robe for three months.' I went&nbsp;
to Miami, and you see the results."

00:03:29.760 --> 00:03:34.960
Funnily enough, even though Murphy turned down&nbsp;
"Rush Hour," he actually inspired the entire film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.960 --> 00:03:40.240
according to an interview the eventual "Rush Hour"&nbsp;
star, Chris Tucker, gave to East Coast Radio.

00:03:40.240 --> 00:03:43.840
"When Eddie Murphy did&nbsp;
Beverly Hills Cop, that's my&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.840 --> 00:03:48.480
Rush Hour. That's how Rush Hour came about,&nbsp;
because of Eddie Murphy's Beverly Hills Cop."

00:03:49.040 --> 00:03:52.960
But despite missing out on all these&nbsp;
blockbuster movies, Murphy has still&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.960 --> 00:03:57.040
only claimed to regret turning down&nbsp;
"Who Framed Roger Rabbit." Fortunately,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.040 --> 00:04:01.040
thanks to his recent resurgence, Murphy&nbsp;
isn't hurting for good roles these days.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.040 --> 00:04:03.920
All these years later, it's&nbsp;
safe to say he's still got it.

00:04:04.720 --> 00:04:06.160
"Yeah, you still got it."

